== Separately installed optional Python dependencies (not redistributed) ==

These distributions are installed beside Camera. Wheels and packages do not vendor or copy them. Camera does not pin an exact version of any of them, and this repository does not record an exact build that the tests imported.

CuPy (https://github.com/cupy/cupy; CUDA 13 distribution https://pypi.org/project/cupy-cuda13x)
Copyright (c) 2015 Preferred Infrastructure, Inc.
Copyright (c) 2015 Preferred Networks, Inc.
Unpinned. operators/sipl_capture_op/python/converters.py imports CuPy, and python-camera.sipl-converters runs when that import succeeds. The operator guide names the cupy-cuda13x distribution and does not select a release. The wheel dependency holoscan-core-cu13==5.0.0 requires cupy-cuda13x>=14.0.0; that floor is the version constraint this release accepts, and no exact tested CuPy version is recorded.
Licensed under MIT (https://github.com/cupy/cupy/blob/main/LICENSE)
The converters module ships in the Python wheel and is imported on demand for Raw10UnpackOp and Nv12ToRgbOp. Importing holoscan_camera leaves it unloaded, and configure skips the converter test when CuPy is absent. A pip install of holoscan-camera-cu13 installs CuPy as a requirement of holoscan-core-cu13==5.0.0. The Debian package does not install it.

NumPy (https://numpy.org/)
Copyright (c) 2005-2026, NumPy Developers, as stated in the upstream LICENSE.txt linked below. The installed distribution is governed by the LICENSE.txt it ships.
Unpinned. Python tests install numpy>=2.1,<3 (python/requirements-test.txt). tools/decode_sipl_frame.py documents "pip install numpy" with no version. holoscan-core-cu13==5.0.0 requires numpy>=1.0.4,<3.0. No exact tested NumPy version is recorded.
Licensed under BSD-3-Clause (https://github.com/numpy/numpy/blob/main/LICENSE.txt)
The converters import numpy.typing, so loading that module requires NumPy. The source helper tools/decode_sipl_frame.py imports NumPy to unpack saved SIPL frames. The V4L2 preview path imports NumPy when preview is requested. The helper is not installed by the wheel or the Debian package. A pip install of the wheel installs NumPy as a requirement of holoscan-core-cu13==5.0.0.

OpenCV, through the opencv-python distribution (https://github.com/opencv/opencv; https://pypi.org/project/opencv-python)
Unpinned. tools/decode_sipl_frame.py documents "pip install opencv-python" with no version, and this repository does not record a tested OpenCV or opencv-python version. Color preview and packed-YUV conversion import cv2 when that distribution is installed; otherwise the helper writes a grayscale preview. The decoder unit test exits 77 when NumPy or opencv-python is missing.
The OpenCV project is licensed under Apache-2.0 (https://github.com/opencv/opencv/blob/4.x/LICENSE).
The opencv-python packaging scripts are licensed under MIT, Copyright (c) Olli-Pekka Heinisuo (https://github.com/opencv/opencv-python/blob/4.x/LICENSE.txt).
A wheel from that project contains the OpenCV library and additional binaries under their own licenses. The packaging inventory is https://github.com/opencv/opencv-python/blob/4.x/LICENSE-3RD-PARTY.txt. Its README states that every wheel includes FFmpeg under LGPL-2.1, and that non-headless Linux wheels, including the opencv-python distribution the helper names, also include Qt 5 under LGPL-3.0, together with the other components listed in that inventory. Those terms apply to the wheel the user installs. Camera does not redistribute OpenCV, the opencv-python wheel, FFmpeg, Qt, or the other codecs and libraries named there. The helper remains a source-tree tool and is not part of the wheel or the Debian package.

